{"text": "ponder", "etymologies": [{"etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"enm\">ponderen</i>, from Old French <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"fro\">ponderer</i> (\u201cto weigh, balance, ponder\u201d) from Latin <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"la\">ponderare</i> (\u201cto weigh, ponder\u201d), from <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"la\">pondus</i> (\u201cweight\u201d), from <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"la\">pendere</i> (\u201cto weigh\u201d); see <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"en\">pendent</i> and <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"en\">pound</i>.\n</p>", "pos": [{"pos": "Verb", "definitions": [{"definition": "To wonder, think of deeply.", "quote": [], "relative": {}}, {"definition": "To consider (something) carefully and thoroughly.\n\n", "quote": [{"citation": "1611, The Holy Bible,\u00a0[\u2026] (King James Version), London:  [\u2026]  Robert Barker,\u00a0[\u2026], \u2192OCLC, Proverbs 4:26:", "text": "Ponder the path of thy feet."}], "relative": {}}, {"definition": "(obsolete) To weigh.", "quote": [], "relative": {}}]}, {"pos": "Noun", "definitions": [{"definition": "(colloquial) A period of deep thought.\n", "quote": [], "relative": {}}]}]}], "pronunciation": [["Received Pronunciation", "/\u02c8p\u0252n.d\u0259(\u0279)/"], ["General American", "/\u02c8p\u0251n.d\u025a/"]]}